l quadratic. If a_0=0, a root is zero and the displayed right side is undefined.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"normalized-root-factors","label":"Normalize the root factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"normalized-root-factors":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_158"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_158"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, y^2=x^3/(2a-x)","reason":"The actual diagram fixes A=(0,0), O=(a,0), B=(2a,0), C above B. With r=AE=sqrt(x^2+y^2) and L=AC, collinearity gives L/r=2a/x. Right-triangle similarity gives AD*AC=4a^2. Thus AE=DC=L-4a^2/L. Eliminating L yields (2a-x)*y^2=x^3, with 0<x<2a,y>0 on the depicted nondegenerate branch. The circle-line scale method instead obtains AD=2ax/r from the second circle intersection and yields the same identity. Denominators and the selected branch are checked in both routes.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q40","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"similar-right-triangles-and-distance","label":"Use right-triangle similarity, collinearity and algebraic elimination","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-right-triangles-and-distance":["cnm_254","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_h173","cnm_158","cnm_036"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_254","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_h173","cnm_158","cnm_036"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circle-line-intersection-and-distance","label":"Find the second circle intersection along AC and eliminate its scale","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-line-intersection-and-distance":["cnm_h176","cnm_h173","cnm_158","cnm_036"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h176","cnm_h173","cnm_158","cnm_036"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1/2+1/sqrt(2)","reason":"The image has J at 