holes gives5, also unlisted.","reason":"The original denominator excludes all its real roots, even when a numerator factor cancels. In a nonpositive set [a,b] union (c,d), a<b<c<d, the closed endpoints a,b must be numerator zeros not excluded by the denominator, and the open endpoints c,d must be denominator zeros. All four endpoints belong to {1,2,3,4,5}. Monicity and degree3 then leave one extra factor in each polynomial. To preserve the required signs and endpoint types, these factors must be the same (x-t): otherwise one gains an extra sign boundary or isolated zero, or changes a prescribed endpoint type. Write P=(x-a)(x-b)(x-t), Q=(x-c)(x-d)(x-t). The choices t=c and t=d are always valid, and define exactly the same function on exactly the same natural domain R minus {c,d}; they must not be counted twice as functions. A third possibility is the unused fifth integer e, but only if its removable hole lies outside the required nonpositive set. This happens for unused e=1,3,5, and fails for e=2 or4 because that hole would cut one of the required intervals. There are five ordered endpoint quadruples, giving five base functions, plus three distinct additional-domain-hole functions, hence eight distinct natural-domain functions. If removable holes are entirely disregarded in function identity, the count instead becomes five; that convention also gives no listed option. The source13 counts polynomial-pair representations: two for each of the five endpoint quadruples plus the three allowable unused-root representations. For a concrete duplicate, (P,Q)=((x-1)(x-2)(x-3),(x-3)^2(x-4)) and ((x-1)(x-2)(x-4),(x-3)(x-4)^2) both define (x-1)(x-2)/((x-3)(x-4)) on R minus {3,4}, with nonpositive set [1,2] union (3,4). The exact certificate independently enumerates all35*35=1225 monic cubic pairs from root multisets, evaluates every real sign cell and root-domain point using rational arithmetic, and confirms13 eligible pairs,8 natural-domain function classes and5 reduced expressions. None of the listed choices is the function count under either stated convention.","status":"confirmed_conflict"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The keyed13 counts eligible ordered polynomial-pair representations, including distinct pairs that define exactly the same function.","Natural-domain function identity gives8; the convention identifying reduced rational expressions regardless of removable holes gives5. Neither count is listed.","confirmed_answer_conflict"],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"signs-domains-and-counting","label":"Count by signs and domains","methodDirectNodeIds":{"signs-domains-and-counting":["math:rational-inequalities","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_158","cnm_h028","cnm_h230"]},"nodeIds":["math:rational-inequalities","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_158","cnm_h028","cnm_h230"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: (x-1)/(x+2)","reason":"[(x-1)(x+1)/(x(x+2))]·[x/(x+1)] = (x-1)/(x+2). The original expression excludes x = 0, -2, and -1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_160_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156"],"id":"cnm_160_q3_m1","label":"Factor and multiply by the reciprocal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_160_q3_m1":["cnm_160","cnm_152","cnm_151","cnm_158"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_160","cnm_152","cnm_151","cnm_158","cnm_156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: x","reason":"[(x+1)/x]·[x^2/(x+1)] = x on the original domain x ≠ 0,-1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_162_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156"],"id":"cnm_162_q1_m1","label":"Simplify inside the parentheses first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_162_q1_m1"